    "hero": {
      "type": "copper-8-myths-hero",
      "settings": {
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/copper-8-myths-hero.jpg",
        "heading": "8 Things People Get Wrong About Copper Water",
        "lead": "Half of what you’ve read is wrong. Some of it is wrong in our favour, and we’re going to say so anyway.",
        "note": "<p>Read this <strong>BEFORE</strong> you buy a copper bottle —<br>or before you throw out the one you own.</p>",
        "author_photo": "shopify://shop_images/copper-8-myths-author.jpg",
        "author_name": "Eric Chandler",
        "author_org": "Neera Living",
        "meta": "Last updated September 2026  ·  3 min read"
      }
    },
    "intro": {
      "type": "copper-8-myths-intro",
      "settings": {
        "text": "<p>Copper water has a marketing problem. It has been sold as a detox, as a cure, as an alkaliser, and as a substitute for half a supplement shelf. None of that is true, and every time someone repeats it, the whole category gets less believable.</p><p>So here are the eight things people get wrong most often. Three of them are myths that would sell more bottles if we let them stand. We are correcting those too.</p><p>And four of them are the reason a lot of people think their copper bottle is broken when it is working perfectly.</p>"
      }
    },
    "myth_01": {
      "type": "copper-8-myths-myth",
      "settings": {
        "background": "cream",
        "reverse": false,
        "label": "MYTH 01",
        "myth_line": "“Copper water detoxes your body.”",
        "headline": "It doesn’t detox anything.\nIt removes one source of plastic.",
        "card_myth_label": "MYTH",
        "card_quote": "“Copper water detoxes your body.”",
        "card_truth_label": "TRUTH",
        "truth_line": "It stops one source. That’s all — and that’s enough.",
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/copper-8-myths-myth-01.jpg",
        "image_headline": "It’s not a detox.",
        "image_subline": "It’s subtraction, not cleansing.",
        "image_style": "default",
        "image_sub_light": false,
        "copy": "<p>This is the claim that put copper water on the map, and it is the one we would most like to be true. It isn’t. No water bottle detoxifies anyone, and any brand telling you otherwise is either guessing or lying.</p><p>Here is what actually changes. If you drink two litres a day, that is roughly 700 litres a year passing through whatever you are holding. Swap plastic for pure copper and you stop adding particles from that source. You are not cleaning anything out. You are stopping the addition.</p><p>It is a smaller claim. It also happens to be one we can prove, which is more than the detox version ever managed.</p>",
        "sticky_trigger": false
      }
    },
    "myth_02": {
      "type": "copper-8-myths-myth",
      "settings": {
        "background": "cream-alt",
        "reverse": true,
        "label": "MYTH 02",
        "myth_line": "“A copper bottle that goes dark is dirty or defective.”",
        "headline": "Darkening is the proof it’s real copper.",
        "card_myth_label": "MYTH",
        "card_quote": "“It went dark, so it’s ruined.”",
        "card_truth_label": "TRUTH",
        "truth_line": "A bottle that never darkens isn’t copper.",
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/copper-8-myths-myth-02.jpg",
        "image_headline": "Patina is proof.",
        "image_subline": "",
        "image_style": "default",
        "image_sub_light": false,
        "copy": "<p>Around week three your bottle starts to change. Brown first, then patches closer to black, and the inside loses its brightness. This is the single most common reason people contact a copper brand, and it is always about a bottle that is working exactly as intended.</p><p>Copper does not rust. It oxidises: it reacts with air and water and grows a patina. Temple bells do it. A French copper pan in a kitchen for eighty years does it. It is the same reaction.</p><p>The bottles that stay factory-bright for a year are the ones worth worrying about. Either they are lacquered inside, or there isn’t enough copper on them to react with anything.</p>",
        "sticky_trigger": false
      }
    },
    "myth_03": {
      "type": "copper-8-myths-myth",
      "settings": {
        "background": "cream",
        "reverse": false,
        "label": "MYTH 03",
        "myth_line": "“All copper bottles are made of copper.”",
        "headline": "Many are plated steel, or lined with plastic inside.",
        "card_myth_label": "MYTH",
        "card_quote": "“Copper is copper.”",
        "card_truth_label": "TRUTH",
        "truth_line": "Plated outside. Plastic inside. Neither is copper.",
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/copper-8-myths-myth-03.jpg",
        "image_headline": "Copper outside. Plastic inside.",
        "image_subline": "Cut one open and you can see it.",
        "image_style": "default",
        "image_sub_light": false,
        "copy": "<p>“Copper” is not a regulated word on drinkware. It can describe a colour, a finish, a plating or a solid metal — four different products sold under one word, with the distinction buried in the specification block.</p><p>Two things happen at the cheap end. Some bottles are a copper skin over steel or aluminium. Others are real copper with a lacquer or epoxy film sprayed on the inside so they never tarnish — which means the water never touches copper at all. You paid for copper and you are drinking out of a polymer.</p><p>Neera is bare copper inside and out: 99.9% pure, food grade, lab-tested after every batch, certificate published on the product page rather than produced on request.</p>",
        "sticky_trigger": false
      }
    },
