    "myth_04": {
      "type": "copper-8-myths-myth",
      "settings": {
        "background": "cream-alt",
        "reverse": true,
        "label": "MYTH 04",
        "myth_line": "“You can put any drink in a copper bottle.”",
        "headline": "Plain cool water only. Nothing acidic, nothing fizzy, nothing hot.",
        "card_myth_label": "MYTH",
        "card_quote": "“It’s a water bottle, anything goes.”",
        "card_truth_label": "TRUTH",
        "truth_line": "Plain, cool, still water. Nothing else.",
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/copper-8-myths-myth-04.jpg",
        "image_headline": "Water. Only water.",
        "image_subline": "Acid and copper don’t mix.",
        "image_style": "default",
        "image_sub_light": false,
        "copy": "<p>This is the one that gets people into trouble. Lemon water, citrus, electrolyte tablets and anything carbonated all react with copper. Hot drinks are not what the vessel is for either.</p><p>The rule is four words long: plain, cool, still water. Coffee, tea and your lemon-and-ginger routine belong in the steel bottle you already own.</p><p>We would rather tell you this on the page you are reading than in a support email three weeks after you bought it.</p>",
        "sticky_trigger": false
      }
    },
    "myth_05": {
      "type": "copper-8-myths-myth",
      "settings": {
        "background": "cream",
        "reverse": false,
        "label": "MYTH 05",
        "myth_line": "“The longer the water sits in copper, the better.”",
        "headline": "Six to eight hours. Not days.",
        "card_myth_label": "MYTH",
        "card_quote": "“Longer is better.”",
        "card_truth_label": "TRUTH",
        "truth_line": "6–8 hours. A night, and no more.",
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/copper-8-myths-myth-05.jpg",
        "image_headline": "6–8 hours",
        "image_subline": "Fill at night. Drink in the morning.",
        "image_style": "large",
        "image_sub_light": true,
        "copy": "<p>Somewhere along the way “rest it overnight” turned into “the longer the better”, and people started leaving water in copper bottles for two or three days.</p><p>The window is six to eight hours — a night, basically. That is the Ayurvedic practice of Tamra Jal, and it is the same window the research used: in a 2012 study, water deliberately contaminated with bacteria was stored in copper pots for 16 hours at room temperature, and the bacteria could no longer be recovered afterwards.</p><p>Longer than twelve hours adds nothing. Fill it when you turn the kitchen light off, drink it in the morning, rinse, repeat.</p>",
        "sticky_trigger": true
      }
    },
    "myth_06": {
      "type": "copper-8-myths-myth",
      "settings": {
        "background": "cream-alt",
        "reverse": true,
        "label": "MYTH 06",
        "myth_line": "“Copper alkalizes your water.”",
        "headline": "It doesn’t. And you should be suspicious of anyone who says it does.",
        "card_myth_label": "MYTH",
        "card_quote": "“Copper alkalizes your water.”",
        "card_truth_label": "TRUTH",
        "truth_line": "It doesn’t. We’re removing the claim from our own site.",
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/copper-8-myths-myth-06.jpg",
        "image_headline": "No, it doesn’t.",
        "image_subline": "Even we had this wrong.",
        "image_style": "default",
        "image_sub_light": false,
        "copy": "<p>You will find this claim on a lot of copper bottle listings, including some very reputable ones. It does not hold up: storing water in copper does not meaningfully change its pH.</p><p>We are saying this knowing it appears on our own website, and we are removing it there too.</p><p>What copper actually does is narrower and better documented: it is naturally antimicrobial, which is why copper alloys are EPA-registered as antimicrobial surfaces and why hospitals fit copper to door handles and bed rails. That is a real property. It does not need an invented one stapled to it.</p>",
        "sticky_trigger": false
      }
    },
    "myth_07": {
      "type": "copper-8-myths-myth",
      "settings": {
        "background": "cream",
        "reverse": false,
        "label": "MYTH 07",
        "myth_line": "“A copper bottle can replace your copper supplement.”",
        "headline": "It gives you a trace. It is not a supplement.",
        "card_myth_label": "MYTH",
        "card_quote": "“It replaces your supplements.”",
        "card_truth_label": "TRUTH",
        "truth_line": "A trace, not a dose.",
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/copper-8-myths-myth-07.jpg",
        "image_headline": "A trace. Not a dose.",
        "image_subline": "0.9 mg a day is what your body needs.",
        "image_style": "default",
        "image_sub_light": false,
        "copy": "<p>Copper is genuinely an essential mineral — your body needs about 0.9 mg a day, cannot manufacture it, and uses it in iron metabolism, connective tissue and energy production.</p><p>Water rested in a copper vessel picks up a trace amount. A trace is not a dose, and a bottle is not a supplement. If a doctor has told you that you are deficient in something, a water bottle is not the answer.</p><p>What a copper bottle is good at is much more boring: it is the vessel you refill a thousand times a year, and it adds nothing artificial while doing it.</p>",
        "sticky_trigger": false
      }
    },
    "myth_08": {
      "type": "copper-8-myths-myth",
      "settings": {
        "background": "cream-alt",
        "reverse": true,
        "label": "MYTH 08",
        "myth_line": "“Copper bottles are high maintenance.”",
        "headline": "One minute a week, with the brush that came in the box.",
        "card_myth_label": "MYTH",
        "card_quote": "“Too much upkeep.”",
        "card_truth_label": "TRUTH",
        "truth_line": "Lemon, salt, 60 seconds.",
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/copper-8-myths-myth-08.jpg",
        "image_headline": "60 seconds",
        "image_subline": "Lemon, salt, brush. Once a week.",
        "image_style": "large",
        "image_sub_light": false,
        "copy": "<p>People imagine polishing cloths and special compounds. The actual routine is lemon, salt and a minute.</p><p>One tablespoon of salt and the juice of half a lemon go in the bottle. Fill halfway with warm water, cap it, shake for thirty to sixty seconds, run the copper care brush around the inside, rinse. No soap, no chemicals, ever. Once a week, or whenever you want the shine back.</p><p>The brush and the travel sleeve are in the box, because you should not have to go and source a cleaning kit for something you just bought. The only real rules: never the dishwasher, never the fridge, no abrasives.</p>",
        "sticky_trigger": false
      }
    },
