    "faq": {
      "type": "copper-8-myths-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is it safe to drink from copper every day?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, with plain water and normal use. The EPA action level for copper in drinking water is 1.3 mg/L; water rested overnight in a copper bottle sits comfortably under it, and your body needs around 0.9 mg of copper a day anyway.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why does the water taste slightly different?",
            "answer": "<p>That faint mineral note is the copper. Most people stop noticing within a week and many come to prefer it. If it tastes strong, shorten the rest to four hours and work up.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "My bottle passed the magnet test. Does that mean it’s pure copper?",
            "answer": "<p>Not by itself. Brass, bronze and aluminium are all non-magnetic, so a plated aluminium bottle passes too. Check the interior colour and ask for a composition report.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I put it in the dishwasher?",
            "answer": "<p>No. Heat and detergent strip the interior. Hand wash only, and never leave it in the fridge — it will sweat.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_5": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How long does one bottle last?",
            "answer": "<p>Copper does not degrade the way plastic does. With the weekly lemon-and-salt routine it serves for years, and in a lot of families it gets handed on.</p>"
          }
